#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e37cf65c-3ffd-5871-81eb-86a8f2a13aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37cf65c-3ffd-5871-81eb-86a8f2a13aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a0c417c-0a3c-5cb0-8980-2828d7552884" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc9ee3ab-a4ec-5015-a591-0ae19d19d9d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332266" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12x-13.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -12(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-13\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha x+(\\alpha -12\\beta )=12x-13.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -12\\beta =-13\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef49e4d0-03df-5674-8144-b982a7a45316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301229} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7d4a37-ff77-5e7c-8b1e-63a23a918bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef49e4d0-03df-5674-8144-b982a7a45316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301229} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3ae9e1-2798-53dc-a38f-5465ec956d54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "069198d4-e282-5b46-ba93-6cbba62d1687" :question/math-academy-id "q-332302" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709ba230-de60-5640-a3b1-3fac092014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x/5}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709ba230-de60-5640-a3b1-3fac092014b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x/5}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275} :answer-field/id #uuid "52bc54ab-ed20-5e27-b5c2-8e5ab134ae4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0437cac1-0430-51b8-a73f-f9f901b83c52" :question/math-academy-id "q-332547" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x/5},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x/5}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x/5}-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf225a9f-68f4-5c7b-87f3-dfde75d31e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308633} {:answer/id #uuid "dd622a14-745d-5bc4-82e9-e678a61adfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308634} {:answer/id #uuid "7a20d413-26f0-53ee-bc1a-c4257a359af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf58d12-f56b-5546-befb-36d9d93221da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c0e438-2e2c-5a8e-abc3-67f8997c9f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21fc98d5-a07a-53e9-b823-65887e0c4938" :question/math-academy-id "q-332552" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351a888f-ff0d-5b36-9dad-1269415b1d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{6x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351a888f-ff0d-5b36-9dad-1269415b1d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{6x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301275} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2350d3-6b2c-559b-a260-10a5fa8d1783" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23d16a30-c940-5f1b-8608-970838c99c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-6\\beta =12\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =0 \\\\ A-2 & =0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\,{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$"}]}
